# Cases whose correct result is known but which KlongPy does not produce yet.
# Each has `: reason` and `: actual` (what KlongPy answers now). The expectation is the correct result.
# `python conformance/run.py --pending` runs them and reports any that now pass: move those to cases/.

@ pending.where-of-empty-list core
> &[]
= []
: reason Where of an empty list raises a type error; the result of `&` on `[]` is the empty list (nothing is selected), as for every other monad on `[]`.
: actual error: type

@ pending.find-symbol-in-list core
> [:a :b]?:b
= [1]
: reason Find with a symbol in a list of symbols returns [] although `[:a :b]=:b` is `[0 1]`; `[:a :b]?:a` is [] too (the string case `"abc"?:b` answers [1] by converting the symbol to text).
: actual []

@ pending.first-of-string-is-a-char strings
> *"abc"
= 0ca
: reason `"abc"@0` is the char 0ca but `*"abc"` prints as the one-character string "a"; the reference suite expects the char (it passes only because `~` treats 0ca and "a" as equal).
: actual "a"

@ pending.first-of-reversed-string-is-a-char strings
> *|"hello"
= 0co
: reason Same as first-of-string-is-a-char.
: actual "o"

@ pending.scan-join-over-string-first-item-is-a-char strings
> ,\"abc"
= [0ca "ab" "abc"]
: reason The first item of the scan is a char in the reference suite (`t(",\\\"abc\""; ...; [0ca "ab" "abc"])`); KlongPy gives the string "a".
: actual ["a" "ab" "abc"]

@ pending.dict-at-string-key dicts strings
> d:::{["a" 1]}
> d@"a"
= 1
: reason `d@k` with a string key returns the whole dictionary; with an integer key it returns the value. The reference defines lookup as `d?k`; if `@` is supported on dictionaries it must agree for every key type.
: actual :{["a" 1]}

@ pending.dict-at-symbol-key dicts
> d:::{[:k 5]}
> d@:k
= 5
: reason Same as dict-at-string-key, symbol key.
: actual :{[:k 5]}

@ pending.dict-at-missing-int-key dicts
> d:::{[1 2]}
> d@7
= :undefined
: reason `d@7` for a missing key raises an internal error (a bug in KlongPy); `d?7` answers :undefined and docs say dictionary lookups are not range checked.
: actual error: internal

@ pending.index-by-float core float
> [1 2 3]@1.0
!type
: reason Indexing with a real returns the whole list; an index must be an integer, so this is a type error.
: actual [1 2 3]

@ pending.reciprocal-of-string strings
> %"a"
!type
: reason Reciprocal of a string is reported as a range error; every other monad on a string operand (`-"a"`) reports type.
: actual error: range

@ pending.where-of-string strings
> &"a"
!type
: reason Where of a string is reported as a range error; the operand has the wrong type.
: actual error: range

@ pending.format-of-dict dicts strings
> $:{[1 2]}
= ":{[1 2]}"
: reason `$` of a dictionary gives the Python repr "{1: 2}"; it should give the written form, as `$` of a list gives.
: actual "{1: 2}"

@ pending.strict2-undefined-name-at-top-level core strict2
> foo
!name
: reason Level 2: reading a name that is not defined is a name error. Level 2 is not implemented yet (ticket 051): the result is :foo.
: actual :foo

@ pending.strict2-quoted-symbol-with-variable core strict2
> a::1
> :a
= :a
: reason Level 2: `:a` is the symbol even when a variable `a` exists. Currently 1.
: actual 1

@ pending.strict2-local-read-before-assign core strict2
> {[a];a}()
!name
: reason Level 2: reading a declared local before assigning it is a name error. Currently :a.
: actual :a

@ pending.strict2-extra-arguments core strict2
> f::{x}
> f(1;2)
!arity
: reason Level 2: more arguments than the function takes is an arity error. Currently the extra argument is dropped.
: actual 1
